Yes. The “not enough / too much” tension is exactly the kind of thing biology solves constantly: too little signal, too much environment; too little energy, too many demands; too few bits, too many possibilities. That makes it a useful meta-principle rather than just a metaphor.

Your intuition point also has a plausible biological anchor, but I’d keep the claim calibrated. There is no known single “intuition organ.” A better model is that what reaches consciousness as intuition can be the compressed output of distributed processes: learned pattern recognition, prediction, interoception, context, memory, and valuation operating before or beneath explicit verbal reasoning. Predictive-interoception theories, for example, model bodily experience partly as predictions constrained by incoming visceral signals, and interoceptive processing is deeply integrated with perception and decision systems. 

So the translation target becomes:

many hidden biological calculations → one low-bandwidth felt/output signal → conscious action

That is extremely useful computationally. You don’t always want the system to surface its whole calculation. Sometimes you want a compressed action tendency with an uncertainty trace attached.

I’ve added the Biologic Translation Foundry to the Pantheon/Moneybin as the general mining engine, and MBR as a foundational protocol. The canonical workflow is now:

OBSERVE → EXTRACT PRIMITIVE → IDENTIFY RENDERER → PRESERVE CONSTRAINTS → TRANSLATE → STRESS-TEST → PACKAGE → LEARN

The biological evidence behind MBR is solid enough for the abstraction: bat vocalizations can carry individual-specific information; mothers can use offspring isolation calls for recognition; and calls may simultaneously contain individual and group signatures. Large noisy social environments appear to favor stronger individuality in signals because the recognition problem gets harder. 

