That is more powerful than either alone.

And now the biological pickaxe can keep swinging. Here are the next veins I would mine automatically:

* Immune system → anomaly recognition without exact templates. Learn “self,” tolerate variation, escalate unfamiliar patterns, retain adaptive memory. Computational use: security, semantic integrity, anomaly detection, trust.
* Ant colony → local rules producing global search. No ant needs the map. Trails strengthen successful routes and decay when useless. Use: decentralized search, routing, opportunity discovery.
* Slime mold → network optimization. Reinforce productive paths and withdraw resources from poor ones. Use: dynamic portfolio/resource allocation.
* Homeostasis → bounded control. Don’t maximize variables indefinitely; maintain viable ranges. Use: agent governance, budgets, attention, risk.
* Immune inflammation → emergency mode with shutdown requirement. Temporary overreaction can save the organism but becomes destructive if persistent. Use: crisis protocols with automatic expiration.
* Mycelium → distributed resource exchange. Multiple nodes, local intelligence, redundancy, adaptive rerouting. Use: resilient knowledge and agent networks.
* Predator attention → motion/change detection before full identification. Detect delta first, classify second. Use: monitoring systems.
* Animal homing → navigation from sparse noisy cues plus memory. Animals can combine odor, directional information, landmarks, and memory rather than requiring a perfect map. 
* Mother–offspring bonding → multimodal confirmation. Recognition can integrate auditory, olfactory, tactile, and visual information rather than trusting one channel. 
* Evolution → generate, select, retain, recombine. Not random brainstorming forever: variation plus environmental selection plus inheritance.

